A||||7||BurlyGoat||Clutch Gone Wacko||||9||
Z||000000||BurlyGoat||08-27-2001||03:05 PM||||I just bought an 86 se with a brand new clutch. The old owner told me that it would probably work if I bled it. When I bleed it, the Cluthc petal starts to feel like a clutch petal, and with the back wheels jacked up it works fine. When I try to drive the thing, the clutch slips. One thing I noticed when bleeding the clutch (at the slave cylinder) there was a fair amount of bubbles coming out (no matter how long I bled the thing). I think it is a leak in the slave cylider. Two questions: <P>1) Anyone know how hard it is to replace the slave cylinder and do I need to replace the whole thing or just a seal ?<P>2) Is there any other things I should look for other than the slave cylinder ?<P>Thanks<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||LarryB||08-27-2001||05:25 PM||||It's not that difficult to do if you're mechanically inclined, just pay careful attention of what goes where when you remove the old unit. From the symptoms you describe a failed slave could be at least part of the problem.<P>You can buy rebuild kits but they aren't all that cheap either and the results are variable.  A lot of us here will tell you that if you do get a new one, get a GM/Delco unit only. <P>If you're seeing bubbles its the slave, the master, or the line connecting them.  The order in which I list them is the order in which you'd find the problem, from most to least likely. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||08-27-2001||05:35 PM||||Air in the hydraulics won't cause the clutch to slip. It causes the opposite problem, the car still tries to go with the clutch in. If your clutch is slipping after you release the pedal you have other problems (probably a bad clutch).<P>For bleeding procedures look here: <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/archism.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/archism.htm</A> <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||BurlyGoat||09-05-2001||08:58 AM||||Well the problem is solved. Let me start by telling you exactly what was happening. The clutch was neither engaging fully or dis-enagaging fully.<P>Solution: For the disengagement problem, turns out the clutch pedal was as bent as a boars.....never mind.....Anyway replaced it with one of them shiny new ones and that problem was solved. Turns out the not fully engagement problem was described in the Haynes manual as the friction plate not seating properly, remembering that this was a new clutch I turned the car on 40 or 50 times and screwed around with the clutch and funny enough it now works..<P>Thanks for the help.||||reg||14
Z||000004||BurlyGoat||09-05-2001||09:00 AM||||P.S. Does anyone know where the engineers for this car got their drugs ? I could use some of these to get through the work day.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||09-05-2001||07:43 PM||||What's a normal clutch pedal look like?  Mine's steel; flat bar stock shaped in a wide "C", but the "C" isn't flat.  It's bent slightly towards the passenger side.  I've never heard of a steel pedal bending.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wolfhound||09-05-2001||08:29 PM||||Here are two photos illustrating pedal problems.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ped1.JPG"> <BR>Notice it's not the actual pedal that's bent but the piece that attaches to it and the master cylinder rod.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ped2.JPG"> <BR>This causes the two pivot points to be misaligned and the rod to bend.<BR>Coy ||||reg||1
Z||000007||thomas_l||09-06-2001||09:36 AM||||When I first got my 87 GT the clutch master rod looked just like yours only a bit more J shaped. I think on mine the rod bent fully, saving the pedal from damage. I hammered it back straight and put it back in. No problems to date and that was 5 months ago.||||reg||1
